Schemathesis (Contract & Fuzz Testing)
    Use Schemathesis to hit individual endpoints in isolation with thousands of chaotic, randomized inputs.
    Why: Its job is to find edge cases, ensure your server doesn't crash (500 errors) when given negative numbers or massive strings, and guarantee every response strictly matches your openapi.json schema.
    Use cases: Testing validation rules, error handling, and schema compliance across the whole API.

What is tested using Schemathesis:
    Examples: Sends requests using example values from your OpenAPI schema to verify basic functionality.
    Coverage: Generates requests to cover all documented input variations, including required/optional fields and edge cases.
    Fuzzing: Sends random, unexpected, or malformed data to test how your API handles invalid or unusual input.

Other tools to use: Tool: Dredd, or Postman Contract Tests.

#### Schemathesis + pytest (only schema.yaml need. Don't need open api client SDK!) #######
    Install: pip install schemathesis
    Can be started with pytests:
        pytest tests/schemathesis/test_xxx.py
        or
        pytest tests/schemathesis/test_xxx.py -s (more logs with ex. print)

    Imprtant configuration file: schemathesis.toml ex. for credentails, enable/disable test phases
    [phases.examples]
    enabled = true
    [phases.coverage]
    enabled = true
    [phases.fuzzing]
    enabled = true
    [phases.stateful]
    enabled = true

    From console:
        Run all: 
            schemathesis run openapi.yaml --url=http://localhost:8000
        Run for specific endpoint: 
            schemathesis run schema.yaml --url=http://localhost:8000 --include-path="/api/user_management/token/"
        Run for specific endpoint and only examples, coverage without fuzzing:
            schemathesis run schema.yaml --url=http://localhost:8000 --include-path="/api/user_management/token/" --phases=examples,coverage

    

##### Schematesis tests in pytest #########
TYPES OF TESTS GENERATED / SCHEMATHESIS
    When Hypothesis powers Schemathesis, it generates different kinds of test cases based on four distinct testing phases defined in your OpenAPI schema:

    Examples Phase
    Uses the explicit example values explicitly written inside your OpenAPI schema fields, filling in any gaps with generated data.

    Coverage Phase
    Systematically walks through boundary values for every constraint in your schema (e.g., maximum string lengths, minimum integer boundaries).

    Fuzzing Phase
    Random, Hypothesis-driven input generation (creates edge cases, malformed data, incorrect types, and missing required parameters to look for server crashes).

    Stateful Phase
    Drives multi-step sequences as a state machine (e.g., creating a user token first, then using that token in a subsequent request).


case.call() is purely a network execution step. It only:
    Generates the fake data.
    Sends the HTTP request to your server.
    Hands the raw response back to you.

case.call_and_validate()
    When you use case.call_and_validate() (or case.validate_response(response)), Schemathesis compares 
    the actual API response against the rules defined in your OpenAPI schema.yaml.
    By default, it runs the following specific checks:

    NO SERVER CRASHES (NOT_A_SERVER_ERROR)
        It checks that your API does not return 5xx status codes (like 500 Internal Server Error). 
        Because Schemathesis generates weird, unexpected, or missing data, this ensures your API handles bad
         inputs gracefully (e.g., returning a 400 Bad Request) instead of crashing.

    RESPONSE SCHEMA CONFORMANCE
        It verifies that the JSON/data returned by your API perfectly matches the responses section in your schema.

        If your schema says id is an integer, the test fails if the API returns a string.

        If your schema says email is required, the test fails if it is missing from the response body.

        It also checks if your API returned an undocumented status code (e.g., returning a 401 when only 200 and 400 are in the schema).

    CONTENT-TYPE CONFORMANCE
        It checks if the Content-Type header returned by your API matches what you promised in the schema.

        Example: If your schema says it returns application/json, the test fails if your server returns an error page in text/html.

    RESPONSE HEADERS CONFORMANCE
        If your schema defines specific HTTP headers that must be included in the response (like a custom X-RateLimit-Remaining header), Schemathesis will fail the test if that header is missing.

Available arguments for .include() and .exclude():
    path / path_regex
    method / method_regex
    tag / tag_regex
    operation_id / operation_id_regex
    name / name_regex